#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166443 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7cc1e18-810d-54cd-90d7-354a865105a7" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Expression for the LTE for a Given Numerical Method"}] :question/id #uuid "db842cc3-76cc-5a6c-b293-3c5f21c58002" :question/math-academy-id "e-21214" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x) = x^3,$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x.$" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x)$ to $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x) = x^3.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at a step starting at $x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)=({x}^{3})^{′}=3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment given by the numerical method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at $x_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & =(x+Δx)^{3} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot Δx+3x\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy) \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot Δx+3x\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3}]-[{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot Δx] \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-{x}^{3}+(3{x}^{2}-3{x}^{2})\\cdot Δx+3x\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3} \\\\  & =3x\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166444 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8905c41-cedb-5c7b-81f7-7639c5451174" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Global Truncation Error Given Numerical Solutions to an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "b0f9cdf5-ca84-537a-9b3e-46b7c56729f1" :question/math-academy-id "e-21218" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate a solution to an initial value problem, resulting in the table below. If the exact solution is $y(x) = \\sqrt{x + 2},$ find, rounded to three decimal places, the global truncation error at each step.\n\n| $n$ | $x_n$ | $y_n$ | $e_n$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.414$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.3$ | $1.480$ |  |\n| $2$ | $0.6$ | $1.570$ |  |\n| $3$ | $0.9$ | $1.650$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "The global truncation error (GTE) is the total error accumulated after several steps of a numerical method. The GTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x_{n-1}$ to $x_n = x_{n-1} + \\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\ne_n = y(x_n) - y_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $y(x_n)$ is the exact value at $x_n,$ and $y_n$ is the numerical value produced after $n$ steps.\n\nWe are given the numerical values $y_n$ produced at each step in the table. Now, we compute the exact value $y(x_n)$ at each value of $x_n{:}$\n\n- The exact value at $x_0=0$ is $y(0) = \\sqrt{0 + 2} = \\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- The exact value at $x_1=0.3$ is $y(0.3) = \\sqrt{0.3 + 2}.$\n\n- The exact value at $x_2=0.6$ is $y(0.6) = \\sqrt{0.6 + 2}.$\n\n- The exact value at $x_3=0.9$ is $y(0.9) = \\sqrt{0.9 + 2}.$\n\nLet's add these values to the table.\n\n| $n$ | $x_n$ | $y_n$ | $y(x_n)$ | $e_n$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.414$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.3$ | $1.480$ | $\\sqrt{2.3}$ |  |\n| $2$ | $0.6$ | $1.570$ | $\\sqrt{2.6}$ |  |\n| $3$ | $0.9$ | $1.650$ | $\\sqrt{2.9}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we find the GTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- The GTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{1} & =y({x}_{1})-{y}_{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2.3}-1.480 \\\\  & ≈0.037.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & =y({x}_{2})-{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2.6}-1.570 \\\\  & ≈0.042.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{3} & =y({x}_{3})-{y}_{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2.9}-1.650 \\\\  & ≈0.053.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x_n$ | $y_n$ | $y(x_n)$ | $e_n$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.414$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.3$ | $1.480$ | $\\sqrt{2.3}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{0.037}$ |\n| $2$ | $0.6$ | $1.570$ | $\\sqrt{2.6}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{0.042}$ |\n| $3$ | $0.9$ | $1.650$ | $\\sqrt{2.9}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{0.053}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166470 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77130223-3730-5a35-bd28-d457f079dad3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the LTE for a Numerical Method Given an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "2d820110-30f7-5fd8-a30c-e21d45cc789f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21604" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = -\\sin x, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to approximate the solution using the following method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the exact solution is $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x) = \\cos x,$ compute the local truncation error $\\tau_{6}$ for the step starting at $x=1$ with step size $\\Delta x=0.2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x)$ to $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution is $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x) = \\cos x.$ So, the $y$-value at the start of the step $x=1$ is $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(1)=\\cos(1).$\n\nNow, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at the start of the step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1) \\\\  & =-0.8415.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment at this step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =-0.8415\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & ≈-0.1683.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at $x_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1+0.2) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for the step starting at $x=1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.2)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)+Δy) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.2)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-0.1683) \\\\  & ≈-0.010\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166471 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9477a5ac-7e05-5fa4-9cf3-352cc17f2e1b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Local Truncation Error Given Numerical Solutions to an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "099fbdba-a319-5a75-af75-b2bbdb1b3494" :question/math-academy-id "e-21605" :question/problem "An initial value problem has the exact solution $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x) = \\cos(2x).$ A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to the initial value problem, resulting in the table below, where $y$ denotes the approximate solution at each step, and $\\tau_n$ is the local truncation error at each step. At each step, the exact value is used to approximate the new value. \n\nCalculate the local truncation error at each step. Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $0.925$ |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $0.692$ |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.368$ |" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x)$ to $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the LTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- After the first step, we have $y_\\text{new} = 0.925.$ So, the LTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.2)-0.925 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.4)-0.925 \\\\  & ≈-0.004.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the second step, we have $y_\\text{new} = 0.692.$ So, the LTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{2} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.4)-0.692 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.8)-0.692 \\\\  & ≈0.005.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the third step, we have $y_\\text{new} = 0.368.$ So, the LTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{3} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.6)-0.368 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.2)-0.368 \\\\  & ≈-0.006.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | $\\tau_n$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $0.925$ | $-0.004$ |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $0.692$ | $0.005$ |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.368$ | $-0.006$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8905c41-cedb-5c7b-81f7-7639c5451174" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Global Truncation Error Given Numerical Solutions to an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0394429b-34e0-510f-8b79-654710c53976" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.057" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0394429b-34e0-510f-8b79-654710c53976" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.057" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323831} :answer-field/id #uuid "964e0f2d-037e-5829-bd2e-a7e98fe0f2a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164201} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7eeb4290-f558-55bc-8a24-7b3c1333f53e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.196" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eeb4290-f558-55bc-8a24-7b3c1333f53e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.196" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323833} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad98ac10-9a3a-5440-9446-87da8aabb50e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164202} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b6a4560-4842-53b0-bd1f-f9b75f3ce67c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b6a4560-4842-53b0-bd1f-f9b75f3ce67c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323832} :answer-field/id #uuid "f686d1c3-fc59-5ba1-8e91-519b48f92a2e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ea5249d-c591-59ff-961c-6c274911fad8" :question/math-academy-id "q-331600" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate a solution to an initial value problem, resulting in the table below. If the exact solution is $y(x)={e}^{-x},$ find, rounded to three decimal places, the global truncation error at each step.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $0.762$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $0.558$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.353$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The global truncation error (GTE) is the total error accumulated after several steps of a numerical method. The GTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from ${x}_{n-1}$ to ${x}_{n}={x}_{n-1}+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n{e}_{n}=y({x}_{n})-{y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $y({x}_{n})$ is the exact value at ${x}_{n},$ and ${y}_{n}$ is the numerical value produced after $n$ steps.\n\nWe are given the numerical values ${y}_{n}$ produced at each step in the table. Now, we compute the exact value $y({x}_{n})$ at each value of ${x}_{n}:$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{0}=0$ is $y(0)={e}^{-0}=1.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{1}=0.2$ is $y(0.2)={e}^{-0.2}.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{2}=0.4$ is $y(0.4)={e}^{-0.4}.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{2}=0.6$ is $y(0.6)={e}^{-0.6}.$\n\nLet's add these values to the table.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $0.762$ | ${e}^{-0.2}$ |  |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $0.558$ | ${e}^{-0.4}$ |  |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.353$ | ${e}^{-0.6}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we find the GTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- The GTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{1} & =y({x}_{1})-{y}_{1} \\\\  & ={e}^{-0.2}-0.762 \\\\  & ≈0.057.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & =y({x}_{2})-{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-0.4}-0.558 \\\\  & ≈0.112.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{3} & =y({x}_{3})-{y}_{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{-0.6}-0.353 \\\\  & ≈0.196.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $0.762$ | ${e}^{-0.2}$ | $0.057$ |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $0.558$ | ${e}^{-0.4}$ | $0.112$ |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.353$ | ${e}^{-0.6}$ | $0.196$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9477a5ac-7e05-5fa4-9cf3-352cc17f2e1b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Local Truncation Error Given Numerical Solutions to an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f7954c7-149c-5a45-a1c5-3067587d3842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.004" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f7954c7-149c-5a45-a1c5-3067587d3842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.004" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323835} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d43eedd-db62-5227-954a-99958bba2d66" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164227} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63560c69-c22b-5b0f-a728-6351834c170b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.006" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63560c69-c22b-5b0f-a728-6351834c170b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.006" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323836} :answer-field/id #uuid "51f143e1-2ee5-5564-abd8-729602a1a6b7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164228} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9989fb44-fa2b-5729-8223-91a4908ba2cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9989fb44-fa2b-5729-8223-91a4908ba2cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323834} :answer-field/id #uuid "548f32b0-f2d3-5737-aa6e-f784ce2fa4f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7eec0e86-aa8a-587e-947b-72186b5d0d44" :question/math-academy-id "q-332275" :question/problem "An initial value problem has the exact solution ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to the initial value problem, resulting in the table below, where $y$ denotes the approximate solution at each step, and ${τ}_{n}$ is the local truncation error at each step. At each step, the exact value is used to approximate the new value. \n\nCalculate the local truncation error at each step. Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.25$ | $0.250$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.5$ | $0.483$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.75$ | $0.688$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the LTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- After the first step, we have ${y}_{\\text{new}}=0.250.$ So, the LTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.25)-0.250 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.25)-0.250 \\\\  & ≈-0.003.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the second step, we have ${y}_{\\text{new}}=0.483.$ So, the LTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{2} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.5)-0.483 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.5)-0.483 \\\\  & ≈-0.004.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the third step, we have ${y}_{\\text{new}}=0.688.$ So, the LTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{3} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.75)-0.688 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.75)-0.688 \\\\  & ≈-0.006.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.25$ | $0.250$ | $-0.003$ |\n| $2$ | $0.5$ | $0.483$ | $-0.004$ |\n| $3$ | $0.75$ | $0.688$ | $-0.006$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9477a5ac-7e05-5fa4-9cf3-352cc17f2e1b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Local Truncation Error Given Numerical Solutions to an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e06573c-eaf6-5d76-84c8-93eacd9264f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.029" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e06573c-eaf6-5d76-84c8-93eacd9264f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.029" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323839} :answer-field/id #uuid "40ee0dff-672c-5f81-a5f3-8ef12e16c78b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323838} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e91954c5-dce2-5f81-844c-85a6f507bada" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.030" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e91954c5-dce2-5f81-844c-85a6f507bada" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.030" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323841} :answer-field/id #uuid "b51b91ff-5b54-5855-bbd2-254aad9c76be" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323840} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2d93293-70ae-517c-896d-2c8bb69b1757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.034" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2d93293-70ae-517c-896d-2c8bb69b1757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.034" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323843} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdbf8dd1-2054-5088-9372-3ed7e9f10411" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "995498e0-bf77-503e-9680-de6584532c4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-333197" :question/problem "An initial value problem has the exact solution ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{3}+1.$ A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to the initial value problem, resulting in the table below, where $y$ denotes the approximate solution at each step, and ${τ}_{n}$ is the local truncation error at each step. At each step, the exact value is used to approximate the new value. \n\nCalculate the local truncation error at each step. Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.29$ | $0.995$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.58$ | $1.225$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.87$ | $1.625$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the LTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- After the first step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.29$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=0.995.$ So, the LTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.29)-0.995 \\\\  & =(0.29)^{3}+1-0.995 \\\\  & ≈0.029.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the second step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.58$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=1.225.$ So, the LTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{2} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.58)-1.225 \\\\  & =(0.58)^{3}+1-1.225 \\\\  & ≈-0.030.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the third step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.87$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=1.625.$ So, the LTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{3} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.87)-1.625 \\\\  & =(0.87)^{3}+1-1.625 \\\\  & ≈0.034.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.29$ | $0.995$ | $0.029$ |\n| $2$ | $0.58$ | $1.225$ | $-0.030$ |\n| $3$ | $0.87$ | $1.625$ | $0.034$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9477a5ac-7e05-5fa4-9cf3-352cc17f2e1b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Local Truncation Error Given Numerical Solutions to an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e27ae08c-f0c1-5546-936d-df3d9a1c3bfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.004" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e27ae08c-f0c1-5546-936d-df3d9a1c3bfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.004" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323846} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c315aad-089f-5ee9-aa6f-00c05cb75b90" :