eld-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.22$ | $0.597$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.33$ | $0.509$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the LTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- After the first step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.11$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=0.756.$ So, the LTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.11)-0.756 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+3(0.11)}-0.756 \\\\  & ≈-0.004.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the second step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.22$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=0.597.$ So, the LTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{2} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.22)-0.597 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+3(0.22)}-0.597 \\\\  & ≈0.005.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the third step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.33$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=0.509.$ So, the LTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{3} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.33)-0.509 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+3(0.33)}-0.509 \\\\  & ≈-0.006.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.11$ | $0.756$ | $-0.004$ |\n| $2$ | $0.22$ | $0.597$ | $0.005$ |\n| $3$ | $0.33$ | $0.509$ | $-0.006$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9477a5ac-7e05-5fa4-9cf3-352cc17f2e1b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Local Truncation Error Given Numerical Solutions to an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a04ad584-5bb1-54ce-9f9b-3ef2993622fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323853} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc1095a-11e4-5697-9533-b183f4d1a4fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a04ad584-5bb1-54ce-9f9b-3ef2993622fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323853} :answer-field/id #uuid "d33cb06b-cf66-5469-a413-e563f5a4b700" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323852} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ade7c61d-80f2-5672-93b8-b49e55acd343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.027" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ade7c61d-80f2-5672-93b8-b49e55acd343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.027" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323856} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a73444d-5ab3-5135-b2d3-cd98ee468cfd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323855} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4948c24-4da6-53e9-a383-a7e6041edd15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.031" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4948c24-4da6-53e9-a383-a7e6041edd15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.031" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323858} :answer-field/id #uuid "d69ebb48-98e0-55ad-8de9-76e2ab072c47" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6300f8cd-9ec3-5d1a-990f-980bc3f21415" :question/math-academy-id "q-333210" :question/problem "An initial value problem has the exact solution ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{1+2x}.$ A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to the initial value problem, resulting in the table below, where $y$ denotes the approximate solution at each step, and ${τ}_{n}$ is the local truncation error at each step. At each step, the exact value is used to approximate the new value. \n\nCalculate the local truncation error at each step. Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.17$ | $0.751$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.34$ | $0.738$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.51$ | $0.724$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the LTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- After the first step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.17$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=0.751.$ So, the LTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.17)-0.751 \\\\  & =((0.17)^{2}+\\frac{1}{1+2(0.17)})-0.751 \\\\  & ≈0.024.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the second step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.34$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=0.738.$ So, the LTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{2} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.34)-0.738 \\\\  & =((0.34)^{2}+\\frac{1}{1+2(0.34)})-0.738 \\\\  & ≈-0.027.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the third step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.51$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=0.724.$ So, the LTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{3} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.51)-0.724 \\\\  & =((0.51)^{2}+\\frac{1}{1+2(0.51)})-0.724 \\\\  & ≈0.031.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.17$ | $0.751$ | $0.024$ |\n| $2$ | $0.34$ | $0.738$ | $-0.027$ |\n| $3$ | $0.51$ | $0.724$ | $0.031$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9477a5ac-7e05-5fa4-9cf3-352cc17f2e1b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Local Truncation Error Given Numerical Solutions to an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f2fb74e-4f3b-55a4-827e-4fdcc86d0172" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323861} {:answer/id #uuid "58a5c66b-378d-5c5b-86d6-dd6b9e3addae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58a5c66b-378d-5c5b-86d6-dd6b9e3addae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323862} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c1f9e74-073b-5a69-8c8d-8e76148ba4cd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323860} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c85bbce3-048f-5dc2-9531-0475275d90a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.015" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c85bbce3-048f-5dc2-9531-0475275d90a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.015" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323864} :answer-field/id #uuid "48e20bab-b1ce-528e-87f3-0a21b0ac62cf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323863} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edc24d12-5f7d-5e7a-8f31-9db032a00e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.011" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edc24d12-5f7d-5e7a-8f31-9db032a00e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.011" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323866} :answer-field/id #uuid "def4ed84-067c-5153-affa-bb7bce6530d5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bbaccaee-9e24-5eae-b2d3-dc3203254cc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-333231" :question/problem "An initial value problem has the exact solution ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=\\sqrt{1+x}.$ A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to the initial value problem, resulting in the table below, where $y$ denotes the approximate solution at each step, and ${τ}_{n}$ is the local truncation error at each step. At each step, the exact value is used to approximate the new value. \n\nCalculate the local truncation error at each step. Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.22$ | $1.117$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.44$ | $1.185$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.66$ | $1.299$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the LTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- After the first step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.22$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=1.117.$ So, the LTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.22)-1.117 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1.22}-1.117 \\\\  & ≈-0.012.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the second step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.44$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=1.185.$ So, the LTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{2} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.44)-1.185 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1.44}-1.185 \\\\  & =0.015.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the third step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.66$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=1.299.$ So, the LTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{3} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.66)-1.299 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1.66}-1.299 \\\\ = & ≈-0.011.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.22$ | $1.117$ | $-0.012$ |\n| $2$ | $0.44$ | $1.185$ | $0.015$ |\n| $3$ | $0.66$ | $1.299$ | $-0.011$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77130223-3730-5a35-bd28-d457f079dad3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the LTE for a Numerical Method Given an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a72489a6-97ab-5658-8d66-72775e54d708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.222" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a72489a6-97ab-5658-8d66-72775e54d708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.222" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323869} :answer-field/id #uuid "06e7f6e3-e961-5d60-9037-4f3daa7fa393" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d307a29-b06f-559a-9712-6b37b8d54e46" :question/math-academy-id "q-333255" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={e}^{3x},$ compute the local truncation error ${τ}_{1}$ for the first step starting at $x=0$ with step size $Δx=0.2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${τ}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact $y$-value at the start of the step $x=0$ is $y(0)=1.$  Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at the start of the step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(0) & =3{y}_{\\text{exact}}(0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment at this step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(0)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0+0.2) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.2) \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\cdot 0.2} \\\\  & ={e}^{0.6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for the first step starting at $x=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-(y(0)+Δy) \\\\  & ={e}^{0.6}-(1+0.6) \\\\  & ={e}^{0.6}-1.6 \\\\  & ≈0.222\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323870 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77130223-3730-5a35-bd28-d457f079dad3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the LTE for a Numerical Method Given an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d1706b6-0fed-5bf8-96ce-75e5ae9dd55f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323872} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f53daa-0f06-50be-9aed-0aee325b83ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2f53daa-0f06-50be-9aed-0aee325b83ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323873} :answer-field/id #uuid "c14762d6-a43b-5ab2-83a4-1528c768a196" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06b84e8c-4740-5260-901a-5b6c0f03dd38" :question/math-academy-id "q-333258" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+3,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to approximate the solution using the following numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =(x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{2}+3x,$ compute the local truncation error, ${τ}_{1},$ for the first step starting at $x=0$ with step size $Δx=0.1.$\n\n${τ}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact $y$-value at the start of the step $x=0$ is $y(0)=0.$ Now, let's compute the \n$y$-increment using the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at the start of the step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(0) & =2(0)+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment at this step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =(x\\cdot {y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(0)+{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(0))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 3+3)\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0+0.1) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.1) \\\\  & =(0.1)^{2}+3(0.1) \\\\  & =0.01+0.3 \\\\  & =0.31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for the first step starting at $x=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-(y(0)+Δy) \\\\  & =0.31-(0+0.3) \\\\  & =0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77130223-3730-5a35-bd28-d457f079dad3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the LTE for a Numerical Method Given an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15edbd2d-edeb-589a-8a60-cd3f5cb2be49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.021" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323876} {:answer/id #uuid "77fd10c1-1875-5b8d-ad47-6bbe398a47a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.021" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77fd10c1-1875-5b8d-ad47-6bbe398a47a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.021" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323877} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae1c8b14-e957-593b-a349-a33edb04e876" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb1dc8bb-2bde-5237-aebc-5fd45ae2416d" :question/math-academy-id "q-333384" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-y,\\,y(0)=3\n$$\n\nSuppose we approximate the solution using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=3{e}^{-x},$ compute the local truncation error ${τ}_{6}$ for the step starting at $x=1$ with step size $Δx=0.2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${τ}_{6}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a single step of a numerical method, when the step begins from an exact value. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=3{e}^{-x}.$ So, the $y$-value at the start of the step $x=1$ is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(1)=3{e}^{-1}.$\n\nNow, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at the start of the step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1) & =-{y}_{\\text{exact}}(1) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment at this step is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-1}\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =-0.6{e}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1+0.2) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.2) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-1.2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for the step starting at $x=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{6} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.2)-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(1)+Δy) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-1.2}-(3{e}^{-1}-0.6{e}^{-1}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-1.2}-2.4{e}^{-1} \\\\  & ≈0.021\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7cc1e18-810d-54cd-90d7-354a865105a7" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Expression for the LTE for a Given Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62f8bb6e-91c8-58f1-9031-b312cd765ec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Δx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323880} {:answer/id #uuid "1807d7ca-570f-581e-b357-11115cd0a39d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cdot (Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323881} {:answer/id #uuid "faa08d70-9367-5369-a475-db1de7a6a7bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x+5\\cdot Δx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323882} {:answer/id #uuid "62312486-2c44-520b-8020-06fc4957ced8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323883} {:answer/id #uuid "a00dbdbf-7c51-5dc2-a97c-6706c28c18a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cdot Δx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62312486-2c44-520b-8020-06fc4957ced8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323883} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d4b7b84-26c4-541b-8c5f-70c2c42994dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b34f941-484d-5b1c-ae15-e848e3beb874" :question/math-academy-id "q-333495" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=5x,$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx.$" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a single step of a numerical method, when the step begins from an exact value. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=5x.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at a step starting at $x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)=(5x)^{′}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment given by the numerical method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =5\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & =5(x+Δx) \\\\  & =5x+5\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy) \\\\  & =[5x+5\\cdot Δx]-[5x+5\\cdot Δx] \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7cc1e18-810d-54cd-90d7-354a865105a7" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Expression for the LTE for a Given Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a29414c4-70bb-532b-8a9e-2a3bbd7473c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+Δx}-(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot Δx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323887} {:answer/id #uuid "8b47045a-468c-5622-984a-a60ee880ddbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+Δx}-\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323888} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb94d9a-c9b6-5051-9663-4b22d33f74bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot Δx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323889} {:answer/id #uuid "f9927871-4d16-5bd9-ba67-9d8b6ec5da87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+Δx}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323890} {:answer/id #uuid "1010d035-9c2f-5e23-8d55-7880ae41b731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a29414c4-70bb-532b-8a9e-2a3bbd7473c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+Δx}-(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot Δx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323887} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c74d7f9-4a90-5323-b737-77a4f2ddae01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bae6a64b-b976-5114-bc74-05b9d6b499d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-333498" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=\\frac{1}{x},$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx.$" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a single step of a numerical method, when the step begins from an exact value. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at a step starting at $x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)={(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment given by the numerical method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & =({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δx) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+Δx}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+Δx}-(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot Δx)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7cc1e18-810d-54cd-90d7-354a865105a7" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Expression for the LTE for a Given Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e0b9d37-ca6e-5d3a-bdcf-edb03888c2c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-{x}^{3})\\cdot Δx+2(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323894} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec0539a-7f25-5903-9c89-0d3371a2660d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}^{4}+33{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}+6x+5\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323895} {:answer/id #uuid "9d60b68a-5d31-5510-9828-66495b91d95a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323896} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3f85b2-a75c-56ab-846f-f126d6660a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323897} {:answer/id #uuid "33cabdf6-a90b-5bac-a2ad-530597039bc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-9{x}^{4}-33{x}^{3}-30{x}^{2}+6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33cabdf6-a90b-5bac-a2ad-530597039bc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-9{x}^{4}-33{x}^{3}-30{x}^{2}+6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323898} :answer-field/id #uuid "32e30fb8-5731-5c7d-ba8f-da73f0bcefee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ade79ae-1aca-58de-966f-f85c3ca37b03" :question/math-academy-id "q-333837" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using the following numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =(xy+{y}^{2})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=3{x}^{2}+5x,$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx.$" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a single step of a numerical method, when the step begins from an exact value. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=3{x}^{2}+5x.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =(x\\cdot {y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+{y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)^{2})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(x\\cdot (3{x}^{2}+5x)+(3{x}^{2}+5x)^{2})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(3{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+9{x}^{4}+30{x}^{3}+25{x}^{2})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(9{x}^{4}+33{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2})\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & =({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δx) \\\\  & =3(x+Δx)^{2}+5(x+Δx) \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+2x\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2})+5x+5(Δx) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}+5x+5(Δx) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+5x+(6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =[3{x}^{2}+5x+(6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}]-[3{x}^{2}+5x+(9{x}^{4}+33{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2})\\cdot Δx] \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-3{x}^{2}+5x-5x+(6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}-(9{x}^{4}+33{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}-(9{x}^{4}+33{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-9{x}^{4}-33{x}^{3}-30{x}^{2}+6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7cc1e18-810d-54cd-90d7-354a865105a7" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Expression for the LTE for a Given Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77fc3fbc-924f-5d38-912e-906a8f7cf25c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2}))\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323901} {:answer/id #uuid "49c5dab6-6971-5769-91ad-2d6938d2da34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1))\\cdot Δx+2(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323902} {:answer/id #uuid "02cc6b28-e136-5757-8957-dbfed49d1728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1))\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323903} {:answer/id #uuid "3f90e703-819c-5e1b-ad35-2c8440b7148d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1))\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323904} {:answer/id #uuid "1cb8473f-c98d-5c5f-94fd-1ab6b46deea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1))\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02cc6b28-e136-5757-8957-dbfed49d1728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1))\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323903} :answer-field/id #uuid "70c76bad-2e57-58c7-b994-2eed3bc78a17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "016b2d53-54fc-5841-ab85-38722d1125fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-333838" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using the following numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{2}+1,$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx.$" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a single step of a numerical method, when the step begins from an exact value. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{2}+1.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{y}_{\\text{exact}}(x))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2}+1)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1)\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & =({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δx) \\\\  & =(x+Δx)^{2}+1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}+2x\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}+1]-[{x}^{2}+1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1)\\cdot Δx] \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-{x}^{2}+1-1+(2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1))\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =(2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1))\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7cc1e18-810d-54cd-90d7-354a865105a7" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Expression for the LTE for a Given Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c698b4ba-47ec-54c3-a865-3c67683d7d46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((2x+4)-{x}^{2}-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+5x+5})\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323908} {:answer/id #uuid "968c1317-39ac-508f-8591-d14035a66d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((2x+4)-{x}^{2}-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+5})\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323909} {:answer/id #uuid "4d425bbd-2de3-5c4b-930b-d2414e049c08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((2x+5)-{x}^{2}-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+5})\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323910} {:answer/id #uuid "c500d8ba-d831-5674-bc20-104fc3014615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((2x+4)-{x}^{2}-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+4})\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323911} {:answer/id #uuid "96e71f43-f8c1-5423-b2c6-205bd06e35f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((2x+4)-x-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+5})\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "968c1317-39ac-508f-8591-d14035a66d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((2x+4)-{x}^{2}-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+5})\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323909} :answer-field/id #uuid "73cb0bbf-894c-54c6-8501-3c317d4b85eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ff583fa-30f7-56b6-ba74-4bf1113a84e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-333839" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using the following numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =({x}^{2}+\\sqrt{y})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{2}+4x+5,$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx.$" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a single step of a numerical method, when the step begins from an exact value. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{2}+4x+5.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =({x}^{2}+\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+5})\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & =(x+Δx)^{2}+4(x+Δx)+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}+4x+4\\cdot Δx+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+4x+5+(2x+4)\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}+4x+5+(2x+4)\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}]-[{x}^{2}+4x+5+({x}^{2}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+5})\\cdot Δx] \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-{x}^{2}+4x-4x+5-5+(2x+4)\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}-({x}^{2}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+5})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =((2x+4)-{x}^{2}-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+5})\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323913 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77130223-3730-5a35-bd28-d457f079dad3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the LTE for a Numerical Method Given an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "739b7864-a455-542a-886d-4763f80b8213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "739b7864-a455-542a-886d-4763f80b8213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323915} :answer-field/id #uuid "e23ec364-9eab-5a53-9623-1654db04d22e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "528ea738-7281-5346-970a-6cb694f1ed20" :question/math-academy-id "q-334150" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={e}^{x},$ compute the local truncation error ${τ}_{21}$ for the step starting at $x=1$ with step size $Δx=0.05.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${τ}_{21}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={e}^{x}.$ So, the $y$-value at the start of the step $x=1$ is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(1)=e.$\n\nNow, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at the start of the step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1) \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment at this step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =e\\cdot 0.05 \\\\  & =0.05e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1+0.05) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.05) \\\\  & ={e}^{1.05}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for the step starting at $x=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{21} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.05)-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(1)+Δy) \\\\  & ={e}^{1.05}-(e+0.05e) \\\\  & ={e}^{1.05}-1.05e \\\\  & ≈0.003\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77130223-3730-5a35-bd28-d457f079dad3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the LTE for a Numerical Method Given an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76af2725-b392-5752-909e-0a4cb4cb233e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.507" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76af2725-b392-5752-909e-0a4cb4cb233e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.507" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323918} :answer-field/id #uuid "60acfe35-bd52-5e4a-a3df-0e1d300afcea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "012e6ab9-3abc-5c2c-aa90-a9294d000bb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-334168" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+1,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to approximate the solution using the following numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}(x+{y}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{2}+x,$ compute the local truncation error, ${τ}_{6},$ for the step starting at $x=1$ with step size $Δx=0.2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${τ}_{6}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{2}+x.$ So, the $y$-value at the start of the step $x=1$ is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(1)=2.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at the start of the step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1) & =2(1)+1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment at this step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}(x+{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(1+3)\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & ≈0.1333.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1+0.2) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.2) \\\\  & =(1.2)^{2}+1.2 \\\\  & =2.64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for the step starting at $x=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{6} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.2)-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(1)+Δy) \\\\  & =2.64-(2+0.1333) \\\\  & ≈0.507\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8905c41-cedb-5c7b-81f7-7639c5451174" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Global Truncation Error Given Numerical Solutions to an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a480ca02-118e-5e4f-b9bf-259e6a45d4be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a480ca02-118e-5e4f-b9bf-259e6a45d4be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323921} :answer-field/id #uuid "6888e60b-b0b7-5e37-bf73-d677c8686b12" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323920} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc5a4ac0-9ff0-51a6-8a32-a88a7e27177e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.011" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc5a4ac0-9ff0-51a6-8a32-a88a7e27177e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.011" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323923} :answer-field/id #uuid "3481225c-adba-5e37-898d-b871088c7075" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323922} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9c396da-a4aa-5817-aa57-e6d26f9e1664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9c396da-a4aa-5817-aa57-e6d26f9e1664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323925} :answer-field/id #uuid "72c9158b-3356-5c22-bf60-80ad48e1c6ad" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f442655c-d19b-55c8-8144-02e2a27d866d" :question/math-academy-id "q-334481" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate a solution to an initial value problem, resulting in the table below. If the exact solution is $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ find, rounded to three decimal places, the global truncation error at each step.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.1$ | $0.099$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.3$ | $0.285$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.540$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "The global truncation error (GTE) is the total error accumulated after several steps of a numerical method. The GTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from ${x}_{n-1}$ to ${x}_{n}={x}_{n-1}+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n{e}_{n}=y({x}_{n})-{y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $y({x}_{n})$ is the exact value at ${x}_{n},$ and ${y}_{n}$ is the numerical value produced after $n$ steps.\n\nWe are given the numerical values ${y}_{n}$ produced at each step in the table. Now, we compute the exact value $y({x}_{n})$ at each value of ${x}_{n}:$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{0}=0$ is $y(0)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{1}=0.1$ is $y(0.1)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.1).$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{2}=0.3$ is $y(0.3)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.3).$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{3}=0.6$ is $y(0.6)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.6).$\n\nLet's add these values to the table.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.1$ | $0.099$ | $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.1)$ |  |\n| $2$ | $0.3$ | $0.285$ | $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.3)$ |  |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.540$ | $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.6)$ |  |\n\nFinally, we find the GTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- The GTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{1} & =y({x}_{1})-{y}_{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.1)-0.099 \\\\  & ≈0.001.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & =y({x}_{2})-{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.3)-0.285 \\\\  & ≈0.011.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{3} & =y({x}_{3})-{y}_{3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.6)-0.540 \\\\  & ≈0.025.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.1$ | $0.099$ | $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.1)$ | $0.001$ |\n| $2$ | $0.3$ | $0.285$ | $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.3)$ | $0.011$ |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.540$ | $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.6)$ | $0.025$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8905c41-cedb-5c7b-81f7-7639c5451174" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Global Truncation Error Given Numerical Solutions to an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7651f6dd-b8c0-5125-84d9-e4275e178c03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.010" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323928} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad996ec-2219-544c-940f-62a13623b6e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.010" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7651f6dd-b8c0-5125-84d9-e4275e178c03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.010" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323928} :answer-field/id #uuid "62c0abd2-67ea-5cf7-9da4-6c3ac00f523d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323927} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2014638-1694-57c6-bf50-7cad0de03905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2014638-1694-57c6-bf50-7cad0de03905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323931} :answer-field/id #uuid "c744a3c3-22ba-5907-8561-b196610954b1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323930} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0402ec42-d33a-5fa9-9da0-73b6b2a35416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0402ec42-d33a-5fa9-9da0-73b6b2a35416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323933} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c9f97ba-fdb7-5ea4-8feb-67a72496567b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa71e1dc-bf5a-5b65-a9ea-ac86df2fe15b" :question/math-academy-id "q-334486" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate a solution to an initial value problem, resulting in the table below. If the exact solution is $y(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+1,$ find, rounded to three decimal places, the global truncation error at each step.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $1.010$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $1.040$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.7$ | $1.140$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "The global truncation error (GTE) is the total error accumulated after several steps of a numerical method. The GTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from ${x}_{n-1}$ to ${x}_{n}={x}_{n-1}+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n{e}_{n}=y({x}_{n})-{y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $y({x}_{n})$ is the exact value at ${x}_{n},$ and ${y}_{n}$ is the numerical value produced after $n$ steps.\n\nWe are given the numerical values ${y}_{n}$ produced at each step in the table. Now, we compute the exact value $y({x}_{n})$ at each value of ${x}_{n}:$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{0}=0$ is $y(0)=\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+1=1.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{1}=0.2$ is $y(0.2)=\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)^{2}+1.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{2}=0.4$ is $y(0.4)=\\frac{1}{2}(0.4)^{2}+1.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{3}=0.7$ is $y(0.7)=\\frac{1}{2}(0.7)^{2}+1.$\n\nLet's add these values to the table.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $1.010$ | $\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)^{2}+1$ |  |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $1.040$ | $\\frac{1}{2}(0.4)^{2}+1$ |  |\n| $3$ | $0.7$ | $1.140$ | $\\frac{1}{2}(0.7)^{2}+1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we find the GTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- The GTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{1} & =y({x}_{1})-{y}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)^{2}+1-1.010 \\\\  & =0.010.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & =y({x}_{2})-{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0.4)^{2}+1-1.040 \\\\  & =0.040.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{3} & =y({x}_{3})-{y}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0.7)^{2}+1-1.140 \\\\  & =0.105.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $1.010$ | $\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)^{2}+1$ | $0.010$ |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $1.040$ | $\\frac{1}{2}(0.4)^{2}+1$ | $0.040$ |\n| $3$ | $0.7$ | $1.140$ | $\\frac{1}{2}(0.7)^{2}+1$ | $0.105$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8905c41-cedb-5c7b-81f7-7639c5451174" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Global Truncation Error Given Numerical Solutions to an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "618d3038-1117-5a24-bdcb-754cedc13265" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323936} {:answer/id #uuid "10efe5b7-d142-51c0-a31d-85609e8d592c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10efe5b7-d142-51c0-a31d-85609e8d592c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323937} :answer-field/id #uuid "51dd18ac-ef30-51f3-ab2d-a88877cb35d0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323935} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c71470cb-6ce3-56a8-9168-9d8791343d19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.018" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c71470cb-6ce3-56a8-9168-9d8791343d19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.018" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323939} :answer-field/id #uuid "c36e8055-f017-5deb-9b79-cd0fa003c6bf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323938} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "baab07e0-9db6-5d76-baa5-c518b7e36d00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.020" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "baab07e0-9db6-5d76-baa5-c518b7e36d00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.020" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323941} :answer-field/id #uuid "774f5da2-1696-5e4b-8c21-26af9414b701" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "692557b6-3864-5ab2-8eff-c2021e9a265a" :question/math-academy-id "q-334489" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate a solution to an initial value problem, resulting in the table below. If the exact solution is $y(x)=\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}},$ find, rounded to three decimal places, the global truncation error at each step.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.25$ | $1.030$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.5$ | $1.100$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.75$ | $1.230$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "The global truncation error (GTE) is the total error accumulated after several steps of a numerical method. The GTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from ${x}_{n-1}$ to ${x}_{n}={x}_{n-1}+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n{e}_{n}=y({x}_{n})-{y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $y({x}_{n})$ is the exact value at ${x}_{n},$ and ${y}_{n}$ is the numerical value produced after $n$ steps.\n\nWe are given the numerical values ${y}_{n}$ produced at each step in the table. Now, we compute the exact value $y({x}_{n})$ at each value of ${x}_{n}:$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{0}=0$ is $y(0)=\\sqrt{1+{0}^{2}}=1.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{1}=0.25$ is $y(0.25)=\\sqrt{1+(0.25)^{2}}.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{2}=0.5$ is $y(0.5)=\\sqrt{1+(0.5)^{2}}.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{3}=0.75$ is $y(0.75)=\\sqrt{1+(0.75)^{2}}.$\n\nLet's add these values to the table.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.25$ | $1.030$ | $\\sqrt{1+(0.25)^{2}}$ |  |\n| $2$ | $0.5$ | $1.100$ | $\\sqrt{1+(0.5)^{2}}$ |  |\n| $3$ | $0.75$ | $1.230$ | $\\sqrt{1+(0.75)^{2}}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we find the GTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- The GTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{1} & =y({x}_{1})-{y}_{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+(0.25)^{2}}-1.030 \\\\  & ≈0.001.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & =y({x}_{2})-{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+(0.5)^{2}}-1.100 \\\\  & ≈0.018.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{3} & =y({x}_{3})-{y}_{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+(0.75)^{2}}-1.230 \\\\  & =0.020.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.25$ | $1.030$ | $\\sqrt{1+(0.25)^{2}}$ | $0.001$ |\n| $2$ | $0.5$ | $1.100$ | $\\sqrt{1+(0.5)^{2}}$ | $0.018$ |\n| $3$ | $0.75$ | $1.230$ | $\\sqrt{1+(0.75)^{2}}$ | $0.020$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8905c41-cedb-5c7b-81f7-7639c5451174" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Global Truncation Error Given Numerical Solutions to an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e686e51-d7fe-5722-ad99-d3b8b64a429d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e686e51-d7fe-5722-ad99-d3b8b64a429d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323944} :answer-field/id #uuid "39b2a112-4331-5736-942f-d24de2ecd63a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323943} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e731151-2d96-5bf8-abcd-91f878e9a7bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.077" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e731151-2d96-5bf8-abcd-91f878e9a7bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.077" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323946} :answer-field/id #uuid "4645f098-de15-505c-948a-c10c32c36d5d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323945} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fc27fbf-828a-57ff-b828-d4cb212c86f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.122" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fc27fbf-828a-57ff-b828-d4cb212c86f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.122" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323948} :answer-field/id #uuid "f970a1d7-8d4f-55ce-b3ee-59fe028dcfb8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d512d996-0f89-54b7-9ffc-da88d5ee7b81" :question/math-academy-id "q-334492" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate a solution to an initial value problem, resulting in the table below. If the exact solution is $y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ find, rounded to three decimal places, the global truncation error at each step.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $0.920$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $0.620$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.240$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "The global truncation error (GTE) is the total error accumulated after several steps of a numerical method. The GTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from ${x}_{n-1}$ to ${x}_{n}={x}_{n-1}+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n{e}_{n}=y({x}_{n})-{y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $y({x}_{n})$ is the exact value at ${x}_{n},$ and ${y}_{n}$ is the numerical value produced after $n$ steps.\n\nWe are given the numerical values ${y}_{n}$ produced at each step in the table. Now, we compute the exact value $y({x}_{n})$ at each value of ${x}_{n}:$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{0}=0$ is $y(0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{1}=0.2$ is $y(0.2)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0.2).$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{2}=0.4$ is $y(0.4)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0.4).$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{3}=0.6$ is $y(0.6)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0.6).$\n\nLet's add these values to the table.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $0.920$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0.2)$ |  |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $0.620$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0.4)$ |  |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.240$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0.6)$ |  |\n\nFinally, we find the GTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- The GTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{1} & =y({x}_{1})-{y}_{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0.2)-0.920 \\\\  & ≈0.001.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & =y({x}_{2})-{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0.4)-0.620 \\\\  & ≈0.077.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{3} & =y({x}_{3})-{y}_{3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0.6)-0.240 \\\\  & ≈0.122.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $0.920$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0.2)$ | $0.001$ |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $0.620$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0.4)$ | $0.077$ |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.240$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0.6)$ | $0.122$ |"}]}
